 "intro": "At the Milvian Bridge over the Tiber, just north of Rome, on 28 October 312. You are Constantine, with perhaps 20,000 to 25,000 men, having marched from Gaul and won northern Italy. Maxentius, who holds Rome, has about 25,000, including the Praetorian Guard.",
 "debrief": "In reality, Maxentius left the safety of Rome and drew up his army on the north bank of the Tiber with the river close behind, crossing on a bridge of boats beside the partly demolished stone bridge; an oracle had told him an enemy of the Romans would perish that day. Constantine’s cavalry broke Maxentius’s, and his infantry pushed the enemy back to the river. In the retreat the bridge of boats collapsed; Maxentius drowned, and the Praetorian Guard died where it stood. The famous vision is told differently by different sources: one says Constantine dreamed he should mark a heavenly sign on his soldiers’ shields, another that he saw a cross of light in the sky, and historians doubt parts of both. Constantine entered Rome the next day. The other choices are what-ifs.",
